BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artful Conversations: John Sloan’s “Scavenger Rocks”
DTSTART:20261105T123000
DTEND:20261105T133000
DTSTAMP:20261030T171909Z
UID:2d9751dbee3b02b35498a7b525632b@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join Us Mid-Day for Artful Conversations Take a midday pause a
 nd experience one work of art in depth. Artful Conversations are informal
 \, docent-led gallery experiences that invite visitors to slow down\, look
  closely\, and engage in thoughtful conversation. Each session features a 
 work of art or object that is new to view—whether a recent acquisition o
 r a work newly installed in the galleries. The program begins in the Educa
 tion Room at our Downtown Campus with a brief introduction and visual cont
 ext before moving into the galleries for an extended exploration of the fe
 atured work. Guided by observation\, curiosity\, and conversation\, partic
 ipants are encouraged to share ideas\, consider different perspectives\, a
 nd discover new connections together. This week for Artful Conversations T
 he program will offer an in‑depth look at John Sloan’s Scavenger Rocks
 \, a recent acquisition that deepens our understanding of the artist’s l
 ong-standing ties to East Gloucester. The presentation begins with a brief
  overview of Sloan’s stays at the Red Cottage from 1914 to 1918\, a form
 ative period that shaped many of his most compelling Cape Ann paintings. F
 rom there\, the talk turns to the granite outcropping near Niles Beach\, d
 rawing on contemporary photographic documentation to illuminate the site
 ’s distinctive forms and its role in Sloan’s artistic process\, offeri
 ng a vivid\, tangible link between the artwork and the landscape that insp
 ired it. Dan Duffy and his wife Helene have been Innkeepers at the Beech T
 ree B&B in Rockport for 16 years. In this capacity\, he takes pride in ser
 ving as an ambassador of goodwill for travelers from around the world. He 
 is passionate about showcasing our region’s rich history\, geographical 
 uniqueness\, and artistic treasurers. As a Cape Ann Museum docent\, Dan us
 es his experience connecting with visitors while deepening his own knowled
 ge of the Museum and of the region’s vibrant social and cultural history
 .\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event
 /artful-conversations-john-sloans-scavenger-rocks/2026-11-05/
LOCATION:Cape Ann Museum\, 27 Pleasant Street\, Gloucester\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/artful-conversations-john-sloans-scave
 nger-rocks/2026-11-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Celebrate Our Library! 2026: An Evening of Authors\, Books & Commu
 nity
DTSTART:20261105T000000
DTEND:20261105T000000
DTSTAMP:20261030T171909Z
UID:0c9e258ea0e65e8e72d467a1f10885@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate Our Library! 2026: An Evening of Authors\, Books\, a
 nd Community Join us for Celebrate Our Library\, the Worcester Public Libr
 ary Foundation’s signature event\, for an unforgettable evening celebrat
 ing great books\, the authors who bring them to life\, and the power of ou
 r public library. Enjoy a moderated conversation with three acclaimed auth
 ors: Celeste Ng\, bestselling author of "Little Fires Everywhere"\; Annabe
 l Monaghan\, New York Times bestselling author of beloved contemporary nov
 els\; and Jennifer Givhan\, award-winning poet and novelist. The discussio
 n will be moderated by Stephanie Pasha\, president of the Worcester Public
  Library Board of Directors. The evening also includes delicious food from
  Simply Delicious Catering\, complimentary beer and wine\, and a signature
  drink\, all in support of the programs and services that make the Worcest
 er Public Library an essential resource for our community. We’ll also pr
 esent the 2026 Dr. Richard E. Greene Founders Award to Lisa Kirby Gibbs. T
 hursday\, November 5\, 2026 | 5:45–9 p.m. Worcester Public Library | 3 S
 alem Square\, Worcester Tickets: $175 per person Register now: https://lp.
 constantcontactpages.com/ev/reg/b73xpeq/lp/4a2bbe1b-c28c-40ab-8b26-558829b
 8335f Join us in celebrating our library!\n\nSources: Discover Central Mas
 sachusetts\nEvent page: https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/celebrate-
 our-library!-2026%3a-an-evening-of-authors-books-%26-community/31182/
LOCATION:Worcester Public Library - Salem Square\, 3 Salem St.\, Worcester
 \, MA\, 01608
URL:https://www.discovercentralma.org/event/celebrate-our-library!-2026%3a
 -an-evening-of-authors-books-%26-community/31182/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Daily Guided & Self-Guided Tour of Hammond Castle Museum
DTSTART:20261105T090000
DTEND:20261105T153000
DTSTAMP:20261030T171909Z
UID:5816cb485a14eee3b75ec24751a715@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside the breathtaking home of one of the most prolific 
 inventors of the 20th century\, John Hays Hammond\, Jr. Hammond Castle Mus
 eum is a dynamic STEAM museum\,*blending its historical significance as bo
 th **a historic house museum and a lapidarium. Inside\, you’ll not only 
 discover ancient\, medieval\, and Renaissance architectural elements and a
 rtifacts—including stone archways\, sarcophagi\, grave markers\, and an 
 extraordinary collection of stone and marble works—but you’ll also fin
 d a vibrant museum offering fresh educational and cultural arts programmin
 g. Visitors can explore items from the archival collection that have not b
 een publicly seen in generations\, along with newly curated content and th
 e most up-to-date information on the provenance of many pieces in our coll
 ection. New this season\, the Museum features four temporary exhibits that
  explore John Hays Hammond Jr. as both an individual and a scientific inno
 vator. In the Natalie Hays Hammond Temporary Exhibit Room\, Curatorial Dir
 ector John Leysath presents Spinning Platters with Hammond: Inside the Wor
 ld of Vinyl Records\, on display from April 29th through August 31st. Begi
 nning September 10th and running through the end of the 2025 season on Jan
 uary 4th\, Leysath will showcase” Hammond in Italy: The Love Affair Betw
 een the Hammonds & Italian Culture.”Additionally\, the Inventions Exhibi
 t Room will highlight Hammond’s groundbreaking Videotape Technology duri
 ng the spring and summer months\, followed by Hammond’s ahead-of-their-t
 ime Advertising Signs in the fall and early winter. Listed on the National
  Register of Historic Places\, and a repeated Reader’s Choice Best of No
 rth Shore winner by Northshore Magazine and a Trip Advisor Traveler’s Ch
 oice award recipient for the last three consecutive years\,** you can trus
 t that your visit will be an unforgettable experience. New This Season: Ne
 w Daily Hours and Flexible Arrival Times for Self-Guided Tours Open daily 
 from 9:00 am to 3:30 pm\, with the last entrance at 2:45 pm. The Museum\, 
 Museum Shop\, and grounds close at 3:30 pm. Experience Hammond Castle Muse
 um at your own pace with our new flexible arrival times for self-guided to
 urs. Your ticket grants you entry anytime within designated periods\, ensu
 ring you can arrive stress-free and enjoy your visit to the fullest. Morni
 ng Session: 9:00 am – 11:45 am / Afternoon Session: 11:45 am – 2:30 pm
  Self-guided tours allow you to explore at your own pace. We recommend pla
 nning for at least 60 minutes to experience the Museum’s fascinating gal
 leries\, grounds\, and temporary exhibits. **Accessibility Note: Please be
  aware that at nearly a century old\, the Hammond Castle Museum is not han
 dicapped accessible due to many difficult and winding stairs on the campus
  and within the Museum\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://no
 rthofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammond-castle-muse
 um/2026-11-05/
LOCATION:Hammond Castle Museum\, 80 Hesperus Avenue\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01
 930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammo
 nd-castle-museum/2026-11-05/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fiona Brehony Explores Gloucester Harbor\, Whale Song\, and the Li
 ving Memory of Water
DTSTART:20261105T170000
DTEND:20261105T180000
DTSTAMP:20261030T171909Z
UID:f51bdc6df2e41ff0ea215c00675c78@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:GOETEMANN ARTIST RESIDENCY BRINGS MANCHESTER-BASED ARTIST AND 
 WRITER TO GLOUCESTER OPENING TALK: THURSDAY SEPTEMBER 10 / (RNAC 5-6 PM) O
 PEN STUDIO: THURSDAY 2-4 PM 9/17 (OCEAN ALLIANCE) WALK/LISTEN: THURSDAY 9/
 24 2-4 PM (OCEAN ALLIANCE) CLOSING TALK: THURSDAY 10/1 4-5 PM (OCEAN ALLIA
 NCE) Gloucester\, MA – What can water remember? What stories are held wi
 thin a harbor\, a river\, or an ocean and whose stories are we able to hea
 r? These questions are at the heart of the work of artist and writer Fiona
  Brehony\, the current Goetemann Artist-in-Residence at Rocky Neck Art Col
 ony. Based in Manchester\, Britain\, Brehony works across moving image\, s
 ound\, and performance\, collaborating with scientists\, composers\, histo
 rians\, and communities to create poetic and inquisitive works that explor
 e the relationships between people\, places\, and waterways. During her re
 sidency in Gloucester\, Brehony is turning her attention to Gloucester Har
 bor\, exploring the harbor as both a natural ecosystem and a human-made in
 frastructure. Her work is informed by historian Richard White’s concept 
 of the “organic machine” – a landscape that is simultaneously natura
 l and shaped by human activity. This idea offers a way of approaching the 
 harbor not as a backdrop\, but as a complex living system in which water\,
  industry\, infrastructure\, human communities\, and other species are con
 tinually shaping one another. Brehony’s approach of thinking with water 
 considers water not simply as a subject or environment\, but as a material
  that connects bodies\, places\, histories\, and species. Drawing on Astri
 da Neimanis’s concept of Bodies of Water\, she is interested in the ways
  human bodies are already materially entangled with the waters around them
 . In Gloucester\, this raises questions about how we might listen to a har
 bor as bodies already implicated in its waters – and how whale song\, hu
 man voices\, harbor infrastructure\, and the movement of water might becom
 e part of a shared\, though uneven\, ecological and acoustic body. Her res
 idency includes a collaboration with Ocean Alliance\, engaging with the or
 ganization’s extensive archives of whale song to develop poetic work on 
 the ocean’s presence and the waters shared with whales. Brehony is explo
 ring the ocean as a living archive – one that carries ecological history
 \, community memory\, and evidence of the ways human and non-human lives i
 ntersect. “Water can hold histories that we don’t know how to access\,
 ” Brehony says. “I’m interested in what happens when we listen close
 ly to a place\, and in what might emerge when those acts of listening beco
 me collective.” The residency extends ideas developed through Brehony’
 s ongoing Liquid History project\, workshops and soundwalks approaching ri
 vers as living archives of social and ecological memory\, previously focus
 ed on the River Irk in North Manchester. In Gloucester\, that inquiry expa
 nds from river to harbor and ocean\, where the scale of water – and the 
 number of lives entangled within it – becomes harder to grasp. The publi
 c is invited to a series of gatherings and events during her time in Glouc
 ester. Brehony is an AHRC-funded doctoral candidate researching the River 
 Irk in North Manchester as a living site of cultural heritage and has spen
 t this summer as artist in residence with the Norwegian Institute for Natu
 re Research in Oslo\, developing Water Symphony\, following the city’s f
 jord\, rivers\, and waterways as a medium for exploring urban justice. Her
  work has been commissioned by the Whitworth Art Gallery\, the University 
 of Oxford\, PRiSM at the Royal Northern College of Music\, and Creswell Cr
 ags Museum\, and supported by Arts Council England and the AHRC. The Goete
 mann Artist Residency Program is a program of Rocky Neck Art Colony\, brin
 ging contemporary artists to Gloucester to engage with the community\, exp
 lore the region’s rich cultural and environmental landscape\, and develo
 p new work in response to place. For more information about Fiona Brehony
 ’s residency\, upcoming public programs\, and the Goetemann Artist Resid
 ency Program\, visit Rocky Neck Art Colony at rockyneckartcolony.org.\n\nS
 ources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/fiona
 -brehony-explores-gloucester-harbor-whale-song-and-the-living-memory-of-wa
 ter/2026-11-05/
LOCATION:Rocky Neck
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/fiona-brehony-explores-gloucester-harb
 or-whale-song-and-the-living-memory-of-water/2026-11-05/
END:VEVENT
